<header><p class="enc-kicker">Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">High King of Ireland</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ard Rí</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The High King of Ireland, Ard Rí in Irish, was the title claimed by kings who asserted supremacy over the island's many kingdoms. Long associated with Tara and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ui-neill">Uí Néill</a>, it became a real prize from the late 10th century, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brian-boru">Brian Bóruma</a> of Munster took it in 1002. The last High King, Ruaidrí Ua Conchobair of Connacht, accepted Henry II's overlordship in 1175.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/high-king-of-ireland.jpg" alt="High King of Ireland" width="266" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brian_Boru,_King_of_Munster.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
